## The one rule

`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json`, then runs Prettier (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict, no warnings allowed), `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the vitest unit tests, and {{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" }}builds the site to prove it
still builds.{{ else if eq .Framework "node-cli" }}packs the package and
runs its program.{{ else }}packages the `.vsix` to prove it
still packages.{{ end }} CI runs the same command, and nothing reaches `main` without it
passing and a review. Never commit red.

## Workflow

1. Read the issue, its acceptance criteria, and your role guide:
   `docs/agents/implementer.md` or `docs/agents/reviewer.md`.
2. Read the code you are about to change and match it.
3. Write a failing test first: one per acceptance criterion, or one that shows the bug.
4. Make the smallest change that meets every criterion. One concern per commit.
5. `make check`, then commit with a Conventional Commit message
   (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`).
6. `AGENTS.md`, `docs/agents/` and `.c4factory.yaml` never change. Other protected files
   change only through a Standard change (see your role guide), never in a normal pull
   request. `.devcontainer/`, `.c4factory.yaml` and `.gitea/` belong to the platform.
   `Makefile`, `eslint.config.mjs`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`,
   `.editorconfig`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/style/` and `docs/agents/` belong to the
   Standard. `tsconfig.json` and {{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" }}`vite.config.ts`{{ else if eq .Framework "node-cli" }}`vitest.config.mts`{{ else }}`vitest.config.mts`{{ end }} decide what the checks check: don't
   loosen them.

## Lints

- Fix every finding; don't silence it. An `eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>`
  is a last resort for a false positive: one rule, one reason. Never
  `@ts-ignore`; never a file-wide disable.
- `make fmt` formats; never hand-format.
- Add packages with `npm install --save-exact` (`--save-dev` for tools), and
  commit `package-lock.json`. {{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" }}Vite bundles what the page imports, so
  `node_modules` never ships; what the page imports is a `dependency`, tools
  are `devDependencies`. Prefer the platform, React and the browser's APIs; then few,{{ else if eq .Framework "node-cli" }}esbuild bundles what the program imports into
  `out/cli.js`, so the package has no `dependencies` and `node_modules` never
  ships. Prefer Node's own modules (`util.parseArgs`, `fs/promises`); then few,{{ else }}esbuild bundles what the extension imports, so
  `node_modules` never ships. Prefer the platform and the VS Code API; then few,{{ end }}
  maintained, well-known packages with a licence that fits. A new install
  script needs an `allowScripts` entry in `package.json`: allow it only when the
  package can't work without it.

## Review rules (lint can't check these)

{{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" -}}
1. **Components are pure:** the same props and state render the same output;
   no side effects, `Date.now()` or randomness in render; props and state are
   never mutated. Components are defined at module level.
2. **State:** the least state that works, owned by the closest common parent;
   nothing stored that can be computed from props or other state.
3. **Effects only sync with something outside React** (a subscription, a
   timer, a request, a browser API), and return the cleanup that undoes it.
   Data changes from an event happen in the event handler. A request in an
   effect ignores or aborts a stale answer.
4. **Accessibility:** semantic elements (`button`, `a`, `label`, headings in
   order), every input labelled, images with `alt`, everything usable by
   keyboard with visible focus, status messages in an `aria-live` region
   (WCAG 2.2 AA).
5. **Errors users see** are on the page and say what happened and what to do;
   never only in the console. A failed request leaves the page usable.
6. **Never a secret in the bundle:** every `VITE_` variable and everything the
   page imports is public. Untrusted input never reaches
   `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or a `javascript:` URL.
{{- else if eq .Framework "node-cli" -}}
1. **Streams:** results go to stdout, everything else (errors, warnings,
   progress) to stderr, so the output pipes cleanly into other programs. Only
   `src/main.ts` touches `process`; everything else writes through the
   `Output` that `run` is given, never `console`.
2. **Exit codes:** 0 for success (`--help` and `--version` too), 2 for a wrong
   command line, 1 for any other failure; set through `process.exitCode`,
   never `process.exit()` in the middle of the program, so output flushes.
3. **The command line:** `util.parseArgs` with `strict: true`; every flag has a
   long name, and the help lists it. An error names the bad input, says what
   to do and points at `--help`. A program that reads files takes `-` for
   stdin; nothing waits for a prompt unless stdin is a terminal.
4. **Errors users see** never are a stack trace: `run` catches what it
   expects and reports one line naming the thing that failed; a real bug may
   crash.
5. **Asynchronous work** is awaited or returned, never left floating; a
   long-running command stops cleanly on SIGINT. Files are read and written
   with `node:fs/promises`; paths are resolved from the arguments, never from
   where the program is installed.
6. **Configuration** comes from flags, then environment variables named after
   the program (`{{ upper (snake .Container) }}_*`), each listed in the help and the README. Never
   print a secret.
{{- else -}}
1. **Activation:** the narrowest activation events (`onLanguage:yaml`), never
   `*`. `activate()` returns quickly: no synchronous I/O, no network, no work the
   user hasn't asked for.
2. **Disposal:** everything `activate()` creates goes into
   `context.subscriptions`; anything created later is disposed by its owner.
   `deactivate()` only to return asynchronous cleanup.
3. **Never block the extension host:** no synchronous file reads on hot paths,
   no heavy work per keystroke; analysis belongs in the language server.
   Providers honour their `CancellationToken`.
4. **The language client:** `vscode-languageclient` over stdio, a narrow
   `documentSelector`, the server found in a stated order (setting, bundled,
   `PATH`) and logged; `start()` failures shown once with the fix; a
   `<extension>.trace.server` setting. Never redo the server's work in the client.
5. **Settings and commands:** every setting contributed in `package.json` with
   a default, a short description and the right scope, and listed in the
   README; commands in Title Case under one category.
6. **Errors users see** say what happened and what to do, with a button to the
   fix; one notification at a time, never repeated; logs go to a
   `LogOutputChannel`, never the console.
{{- end }}
7. **Types:** no `any` (narrow `unknown` at the boundary, checked at runtime when
   the input is untrusted); no non-null assertion without a disable comment
   saying why; no `as` to silence the compiler; exhaustive switches over unions.
{{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" }}8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that import neither React nor the
   DOM, tested with plain vitest. A component test brings its own DOM
   (`// @vitest-environment jsdom`) and queries by role and label.
{{- else if eq .Framework "node-cli" -}}
8. **Testable through `run`:** command behaviour is tested by calling `run`
   with arguments and a recording `Output`, checking the exit code and both
   streams; logic lives in modules that don't touch `process`. Tests that need
   files use a fresh temporary directory.
{{- else }}8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that don't import `vscode`, tested
   with plain vitest. Code that needs `vscode` is tested against
   `test/vscode.ts`; extend the stand-in with `satisfies` types.
{{- end }}
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo.test.ts`), `it.each` tables, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests.
10. **Performance:** when the issue or the system's needs state a budget, a test
    or benchmark measures it on realistic input and the pull request gives the
    numbers.
{{ if eq .Framework "react-vite" }}11. **Input:** empty, huge, malformed and non-ASCII input never breaks the
    page; lists have stable keys from the data, never the index of a list
    that changes.
{{- else if eq .Framework "node-cli" -}}
11. **Input:** empty, huge, malformed and non-ASCII arguments, files and
    stdin never crash the program; large input is streamed, not read whole
    when it can be big.
{{- else }}11. **Input:** empty, huge, malformed and non-ASCII documents never crash the
    extension; LSP positions count UTF-16 code units.
{{- end }}
12. **Releases:** user-visible changes get a `CHANGELOG.md` entry under
    "Unreleased"; the version in `package.json` changes only in a release issue.
13. **Names:** `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types, `CONSTANT_CASE`
    module constants, acronyms as words (`HttpUrl`); files `snake_case.ts`.
14. **No dead code:** no commented-out code, speculative options or "for later"
    features; no TODO without an issue number.

